     EXPLANATION OF OPTION
 
     Use this option to modify a drive's status by adding it, deleting it, 
     failing it, or reconstructing its drive data.  
 
     NOTE:  A procedure for modifying a drive status is included in the 
            help screens following the explanation of option.
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
                                  (CONTINUED)
  HOW TO MODIFY A DRIVE'S STATUS
 
   ===================================================================
   HOW TO MODIFY                          |  STATUS BECOMES . . .
   ---------------------------------------+---------------------------
   Add a non-existent (1) drive           | Spare (2)
   ---------------------------------------+---------------------------
   Delete an optimal (0) drive            | Non-existent (1)
   ---------------------------------------+---------------------------
   Fail an optimal (0) drive              |
   ---------------------------------------+ Failed (3)
   Fail a warning (5) drive               |
   ---------------------------------------+---------------------------
   Reconstruct data on a failed (3)       | Replace (4) to Optimal (0)
   drive *                                |
   ---------------------------------------+---------------------------
   Format a failed (3) drive before       | Formatting (8) to
   reconstructing drive data              | Replace (4) to Optimal (0)
   -------------------------------------------------------------------
   * This occurs automatically if you have not disabled the 
     "Auto-detection Of Drive Replacement And Reconstruction" option.
   ===============================(CONTINUED)=========================
   "ADD" DRIVE STATUS
 
     WHAT "ADD" MEANS:  Adding a drive through software causes the array 
     controller to change the drive status from "Non-existent" (1) to 
     "Spare" (2), thus making the drive available for you to create a 
     logical unit.  
 
     WHEN TO ADD A DRIVE:  You do NOT need to use this option (unless you 
     mistakenly delete a drive).  It is a feature for non-NCR array 
     subsystems.
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
                                  (CONTINUED)
     "DELETE" DRIVE STATUS
 
     WHAT "DELETE" MEANS:  Deleting a drive through software causes the 
     array controller to change the drive status from "Spare" (2) to "Non-
     existent" (1).  This means you can never delete a drive that is part 
     of a logical unit.  First, delete the logical unit (after backing up 
     any data on it), then delete the drive.
 
     WHEN TO DELETE A DRIVE:  Delete a drive when you plan to remove the 
     drive from an array subsystem (or integrated array) but are not 
     planning to replace it.  
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
                                (CONTINUED)
     "FAIL" DRIVE STATUS
 
     WHAT "FAIL" MEANS:  Failing a drive through software causes the array 
     controller to change the drive status from "Optimal" (0) or "Warning" 
     (5) to "Failed" (3).  Once a drive is failed, the array controller can 
     NOT access that drive's data until you reconstruct the drive data 
     (select the "Reconstruct" drive data option).  
 
     IMPORTANT NOTE:  Do NOT fail a drive in a RAID 0 logical unit
                      because there is no parity.  Do NOT fail a 
                      drive in a RAID 1, 3, or 5 logical unit if
                      the logical unit is already degraded.
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
                                  (CONTINUED)
     WHEN TO FAIL A DRIVE:  Fail a drive in the following circumstances:
 
          - If the drive status is "Warning" (5) and you want
            to replace the drive.  You should fail the drive
            before you replace it.
 
          - When you want to format a drive in a logical unit.
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
                                (CONTINUED)
     "RECONSTRUCT" DRIVE STATUS
 
     WHAT "RECONSTRUCT" MEANS:  Reconstructing data on a drive causes the 
     array controller to do the following, in order:
 
          - Sense that there is a new drive in the array,
          - Change the drive status to "Replace" (4),
          - Copy special array controller files to the new drive,
          - Recalculate data and parity from the data and parity
            on the other drives,
          - Write the recalculated data and parity to the new drive,
          - Change the drive status to "Optimal" (0).
 
 
     WHEN TO RECONSTRUCT DRIVE DATA:  When you replace a drive, the array 
     controller AUTOMATICALLY starts reconstructing the drive data (unless 
     you have disabled the "Auto-detection Of Drive Replacement And 
     Reconstruction" option using acf).  If you replace a drive and 
     reconstruction does NOT start automatically, manually initiate 
     reconstruction using this option.
 
                                  (CONTINUED)
     IMPORTANT NOTE:  Remember, a RAID 0 array does not have parity so 
                      you can NOT recover the data by reconstructing 
                      it.  You must restore the RAID 0 array to a 
                      working state by formatting the logical unit, 
                      then copying the data from the back-up media 
                      file-by-file (see the "Format Logical Unit" 
                      option in the logical unit window).
 
     ABOUT FORMATTING A DRIVE 
 
     Most of the time, a drive is pre-formatted, so the array controller 
     does not format it again.  However, you may want to format a drive 
     that is already in the logical unit if you suspect that the drive is 
     still good.
 
     First, to force a drive format, you must disable the "Auto-detection 
     Of Drive Replacement And Reconstruction" option (using acf).  Then, 
     replace the drive, and reconstruction will NOT automatically start.  
     Next, select the "Reconstruct" drive data option in the drive window 
     to manually initiate reconstruction, and the Array Configuration 
     Editor gives you the choice to format the drive.  Press "y" to format 
                                  (CONTINUED)
     the drive.  While the drive is formatting, the drive status is 
     "Formatting" (8).
     PROCEDURE -- MODIFYING A DRIVE STATUS: 
     ADDING, DELETING, FAILING OR RECONSTRUCTING
 
     NOTE:  See the other help options for the specific steps to add, 
            delete, or fail a drive, or to reconstruct drive data. 
 
     1.   Select a drive by pressing the arrow keys.
 
     2.   Press a key corresponding to one of the options in the
          drive window.  For example, press "a" to add a drive.
 
     NOTE:  See the "Help" option in the drive window for more 
            information about the drive operations you can perform.
 
     3.   If necessary, press "c" to confirm that you want to continue
          with the operation you selected, or press "q" to quit.
